.TH EJSON 1 "January 2026" "ejson" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ejson \- manage application secrets in source control via encrypted JSON, TOML, or YAML
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ejson
[\fB\-\-keydir\fR \fIpath\fR]
.I COMMAND
[\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ejson
is a utility for managing a collection of secrets in source control.
The secrets are encrypted using public key, elliptic curve cryptography.
Secrets are collected in a JSON, TOML, or YAML file, in which all the string values are encrypted.
Public keys are embedded in the file, and the decrypter looks up the corresponding
private key from its local filesystem.
.PP
See
.BR ejson (5)
for more information on the
.B ejson
file format, and read on for a workflow example.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-keydir= path
Path to directory containing private keys. Defaults to
.IR /opt/ejson/keys .
Setting
.B EJSON_KEYDIR
will also set this value, with lower precedence.
.SH SUPPORTED FORMATS
Format detection is automatic based on file extension:
.TP
.B JSON
Extensions:
.IR .ejson ", " .json
.TP
.B TOML
Extensions:
.IR .etoml ", " .toml
.TP
.B YAML
Extensions:
.IR .eyaml ", " .eyml ", " .yaml ", " .yml
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ejson encrypt
Encrypt one or more ejson, etoml, or eyaml files. Alias:
.BR "ejson e" .
See
.BR ejson\-encrypt (1).
.TP
.B ejson decrypt
Decrypt an ejson, etoml, or eyaml file. Alias:
.BR "ejson d" .
See
.BR ejson\-decrypt (1).
.TP
.B ejson keygen
Generate an ejson keypair. Alias:
.BR "ejson g" .
See
.BR ejson\-keygen (1).
.TP
.B ejson env
Export environment variables from the
.I environment
key in an ejson, etoml, or eyaml file.
See
.BR ejson\-env (1).
.SH WORKFLOW
.SS 1: Create the Keydir
By default, EJSON looks for keys in
.IR /opt/ejson/keys .
You can change this by setting
.B EJSON_KEYDIR
or passing the
.B \-keydir
option.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mkdir -p /opt/ejson/keys
.fi
.RE
.SS 2: Generate a keypair
When called with
.BR \-w ,
.B ejson keygen
will write the keypair into the
.I keydir
and print the public key. Without
.BR \-w ,
it will print both keys to stdout. This is useful if you have to distribute
the key to multiple servers via configuration management, etc.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ejson keygen
Public Key:
63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f
Private Key:
75b80b4a693156eb435f4ed2fe397e583f461f09fd99ec2bd1bdef0a56cf6e64

$ ejson keygen -w
53393332c6c7c474af603c078f5696c8fe16677a09a711bba299a6c1c1676a59

$ cat /opt/ejson/keys/5339*
888a4291bef9135729357b8c70e5a62b0bbe104a679d829cdbe56d46a4481aaf
.fi
.RE
.SS 3: Create an ejson file
The format is described in more detail in
.BR ejson (5).
For now, create a file that looks something like this.
Fill in the
.I <key>
with whatever you got back in step 2.
.PP
Create this file as
.IR secrets.ejson
(or use
.IR .etoml " / " .eyaml
extensions for TOML/YAML formats):
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "_public_key": "<key>",
  "database_password": "1234password"
}
.fi
.RE
.SS 4: Encrypt the file
Running
.B ejson encrypt secrets.ejson
will encrypt any new plaintext keys in the file, and leave any existing
encrypted keys untouched:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f",
  "database_password": "EJ[1:WGj2t4znULHT1IRveMEdvvNXqZzNBNMsJ5iZVy6Dvxs=:..."
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Try adding another plaintext secret to the file and run
.B ejson encrypt secrets.ejson
again. The
.I database_password
field will not be changed, but the new secret will be encrypted.
.SS 5: Decrypt the file (full output)
To decrypt the file, you must have a file present in the
.I keydir
whose name is the 64-byte hex-encoded public key exactly as embedded in the
.BR ejson (5)
document. The contents of that file must be the similarly-encoded private key.
If you used
.BR "ejson keygen \-w" ,
you've already got this covered.
.PP
Unlike
.BR ejson\-encrypt (1),
which overwrites the specified files,
.BR ejson\-decrypt (1)
only takes one file parameter, and prints the output to
.IR stdout :
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ejson decrypt secrets.ejson
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f",
  "database_password": "1234password"
}
.fi
.RE
.SS 6: Export environment variables
If your secrets file contains an
.I environment
key with key-value pairs, you can export them as shell environment variables:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cat secrets.ejson
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a...",
  "environment": {
    "DATABASE_URL": "EJ[1:...]",
    "API_KEY": "EJ[1:...]"
  }
}

$ ejson env secrets.ejson
export DATABASE_URL='postgres://...'
export API_KEY='sk-...'

$ eval $(ejson env secrets.ejson)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use
.B \-q
or
.B \-\-quiet
to omit the
.I export
prefix, which is useful for .env file generation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ejson env -q secrets.ejson > .env
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EJSON_KEYDIR
Directory containing private keys. Defaults to
.IR /opt/ejson/keys .
Can be overridden by the
.B \-\-keydir
option.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/ejson/keys
Default directory for storing private keys. Each key file is named after the
64-byte hex-encoded public key and contains the corresponding private key.
